libya's rival military committees have  been meeting  in the city of sirte for the last three  days  the meetings facilitated by the u.n have  played a critical role  in the success of this week's talks in  switzerland  a nationwide ceasefire was agreed last  october in geneva  which stipulated that all foreign  fighters should leave the country within  three months  but the fighters have failed to withdraw  by the january 24th  deadline and the deal also called for  the main roads between eastern and  western libya to reopen  a member of the joint military committee  says that will happen  once they remove land mines from the  area  we agreed on opening the road between  east and west and we've agreed to start  the process of clearing minds through  the specialist teams that will start  their work on february 10th and end on  february 25th  the un security council has requested  the secretary general to establish and  deploy a team to libya to monitor the  ceasefire  essential that all foreign fighters and  mercenaries  move first to tripoli and benghazi and  then leave the country  according to the new schedule that was  defined  and it is essential that everybody  cooperates with the new authorities to  make peace happen  in libya according to the u.n support  mission there are more than 20 000  foreign fighters in libya  with an estimated two thousand russian  mercenaries  moscow was one of warlord khalifa  hafter's biggest supporters  in his 14-month failed military campaign  to take control of the capital tripoli  the u.s  africa command has said russia flew  fourth generation jet fighters into  libya  with neither hafters forces nor private  military companies  having the ability to arm operate and  sustain the jets  without state support the jets are  believed to be based in the el jafra air  base  in central libya  we agreed to send a letter to the  security council demanding that  countries that have mercenaries and  forces on the ground  must be removed immediately on friday  libya's rival factions agreed to form a  new transitional government  that will lead the country until  elections in december  libya's new unified government has  received international praise and  support  but the road ahead is filled with many  challenges  forcing foreign fighters out of the  country will perhaps be the most  daunting task  but without doing that it could be  difficult to hold a nationwide election  come december malak trainer al jazeera  tripoli
 

